\begin_layout Section*
Axing AXE-N
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Ericsson makes telephone exchanges.
 These have a variety of demanding requirements,
 including (a) not breaking;
 (b) tolerating faults without breaking;
 (c) ‘soft real-time’ i.e.
 not breaking if something is a bit delayed;
 (d) running non-stop without breaking;
 and (e) the ability to change an entire program while it is still running,
 without breaking.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By pretty much any measure,
 the Ellemtel AXE-N project was a huge failure.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "On their website"
target "https://www.ericsson.com/en/about-us/history/changing-the-world/the-nordics-take-charge/ellemtel-and-the-axe"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 Ericsson describes how L.M.
 Ericsson and Televerket set up Ellemtel as a successful joint venture in the 1960s.
 They came up with the successful 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "AXE"
target "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AXE_telephone_exchange"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 electronic switching system in the 1970s.
 AXE-N was to be its successor,
 a single,
 universal,
 global telecommunications system.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At least,
 in theory.
 Asynchronous Transfer Mode (ATM
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Don’t confuse this with automated teller machines.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset

) tech was to be used to build something remarkably similar to the Internet,
 but for all telecoms.
 The project started in 1987,
 and SEK 10 billion later,
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
North of a billion USD,
 one of the largest failed telecom projects ever,
 in any currency.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "died in ignominy"
target "https://www.ericsson.com/en/about-us/history/changing-the-world/big-bang/axe-n"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 in December 1995.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The key change here was a move from Ericsson’s own in-house PLEX programming language to the “industrial standard programming language” C++.
 The problem with complex projects that die is that they are complex,
 and so it’s easy to find a thousand reasons for failure.
 The big change to an “industrial standard” can be rationalised as “not the real problem”.
 We all know that complex projects fail for other reasons—
overreach,
 bad management,
 poor synchronisation of the moving parts,
 and excessive complexity itself.
 A scapegoat can be found.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Such failures are less forgivable when the real defect can be found and replaced,
 with subsequent astounding success—
and the error is then repeated!
 First,
 let’s look at the fix.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Erlang
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are two complementary origins for the name ‘Erlang’.
 It’s a convenient portmanteau of the initial syllables of ‘Ericsson’ and ‘Language’;
 but it also refers to the Danish telecommunications pioneer 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Agner Krarup Erlang"
target "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agner_Krarup_Erlang"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 who invented queueing theory.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Fortunately for Ericsson,
 when AXE-N failed,
 Erlang had been waiting in the wings,
 under development for a decade—
in house—
by Joe Armstrong and a handful of other engineers.
 Erlang is a functional language.
 The fundamental idea behind a ‘functional language’ is that it is made up of ‘pure functions’.
 These are functions because they take zero or more pieces of information (‘arguments’) and produce a single result.
 They are pure because they produce no side effects.
 For telecommunications,
 this is a very good thing.
 Erlang has other good things.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Erlang is designed for telecoms.
 Its key features are (a) not breaking;
 (b) tolerating faults without breaking;
 (c) ‘soft real-time’ i.e.
 not breaking if something is a bit delayed;
 (d) running nonstop without breaking;
 and (e) the ability to change an entire program while it is still running,
 without breaking.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Over the next 3 years,
 Ericsson invested a lot of face-saving resources into repairing the AXE-N debacle.
 The Erlang team was expanded from 2 to over 60 people,
 who commandeered the existing hardware and by 1998 produced and then released the AXD301 ATM switch—
with the aforementioned,
 legendary performance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
And now for the fun bit.
 Also in 1998,
 Ericsson forbade the use of Erlang in future projects (favouring C++).
 In December 1998,
 Erlang was open-sourced and most of the Erlang team resigned.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Subsequently"
target "https://erlang.org/pipermail/erlang-questions/2006-July/021368.html"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 \SpecialChar ldots

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In 1999,
 management at Erlang suddenly stopped having ‘problems’.
 These were replaced by ‘issues’.
 Issues then rapidly transformed into ‘challenges’,
 which in turn gave way to ‘opportunities’.
 Their opportunities became so great that in 2004,
 Ericsson re-hired Armstrong and—
it is said—
implemented a “don’t ask permission,
 just use it” approach to Erlang.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One would hope they paid him well.
\end_layout

