\begin_layout Section*
What can we learn?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider the 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "above graphic from 2002"
target "https://guug.de/veranstaltungen/ffg2003/papers/ffg2003-armstrong.pdf"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 At the top,
 in red,
 we have success.
 An Erlang-based web server (Yaws) sustains communication at around 800 KB/second with 80,000 interrupting processes,
 running on NFS (the Network File System).
 On the left in blue,
 the more conventional Apache runs at half that communication rate,
 and in this same environment,
 chokes up and dies with about 4000 interrupting processes.
 The green is running on the local machine,
 rather than NFS.
 It also dies soon.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Armstrong calls this ‘Concurrency-oriented programming’.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Everything is simply designed around communicating processes.
 Built into the design are small,
 light,
 independent processes that deliberately don’t have side effects.
 All interaction is through asynchronous messages.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Different readers will take away different lessons.
 Some might reasonably conclude “If you need fault-tolerant concurrency,
 favour Erlang over C++ in many circumstances”.
 I can however see some more general lessons.
 These relate to:
