\begin_layout Section
The worst decision
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are multiple candidates for the worst idea in computer programming.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "GOTO statements"
target "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Considered_harmful"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 that invite “spaghetti programming”.
 Tight coupling between modules,
 or between 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "the information layer and the functional layer"
target "https://drjo.substack.com/i/184290811/but-monoliths-work"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
⌘
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Failing to embrace 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "literate programming."
target "https://drjo.substack.com/p/are-you-literate"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
⌘
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Insightless use of 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "NoSQL"
target "https://drjo.substack.com/i/160166306/but-no-sql"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
⌘
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Excessive code complexity.
 But here’s an example of my #1 for defective code:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\family typewriter
N = N + 1;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I’m sure at least some readers are thinking “C’mon,
 why’s that so bad then?
 All we have here is a box (called ‘N’) containing a value,
 and all we’re doing is adding one.
 This is standard coding.
 What could possibly be so bad about this?” Some programmers will be even more forthright.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider instead a language where a ‘variable’ can only ever be written once:
 it’s either undefined,
 or it has a constant value.
 For me,
 this sort of decision about the ‘flavour’ of a language is fundamental,
 important and profound.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From now on,
 let’s refer to such single-assignment variables as “WORM”—
Write Once,
 Read Many.
 You’ll meet this in Erlang,
 Rust and 
\series bold
\emph on
Zyzyxia
\series default
\emph default
—
and then squirrelled away in many other languages.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Although Rust buggers things up by (of course) adding a ‘mut’ option.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset

 For example,
 in modern JavaScript,
 we have the option of 
\family typewriter
const N = 123
\family default
;
 —
 but it still supports 
\family typewriter
var
\family default
,
 embraces 
\family typewriter
let
\family default
 —
 and 
\family typewriter
const
\family default
 objects can be modified!
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
WORM turns you.
 It changes the entire way you program.
 It constrains the way you use state.
 The first and most obvious impact is on globals.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Globals
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Good programmers rightly disparage globals that are mutable.
 There are two distinct issues here.
 The first relates to one of those big problems in all programs—
naming.
 If you choose names badly,
 nothing will help you.
 This is more a social problem than a programming one.
 If you are using double-precision floating point numbers,
 then setting 
\family typewriter
Pi
\family default
 to anything other than 
\family typewriter
3.141592653589793~
\family default
 is just silly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second problem comes when some well-meaning person
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Or idiot.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset

 re-defines a global value.
 This is still a social problem related to communicating numerical values,
 but it rapidly becomes a practical problem of things failing,
 sometimes rather intermittently.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
WORM mitigates the first problem and eliminates the second.
 If I say \SpecialChar ldots

\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation

\family typewriter
SET <Phi> 1.618033988749895~
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation

\family typewriter
SET <Hello> <Hello,
 world!>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\SpecialChar ldots
 in my language 
\series bold
\emph on
Zyzyxia
\series default
\emph default
,
 then these values are immutable.
 In fact,
 I can’t even re-define a verb once I’ve SET its value.
 And with this approach,
 globals lose all of their terror.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By making each global immutable,
 you do three things.
 You decrease complexity,
 because there are just two options:
 undefined,
 or that constant value.
 You substantially decrease the problem of 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "mutable state"
target "https://softwareengineering.stackexchange.com/questions/235558/what-is-state-mutable-state-and-immutable-state"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 But most important,
 you alter how people think about programming.
 Let’s try an example.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Local variables
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To calculate the period of a pendulum accurately,
 it’s useful to be able to work out the arithmetic-geometric mean or agm.
 That’s because a pretty precise period for a pendulum is given by 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "this equation"
target "http://leapsecond.com/hsn2006/pendulum-period-agm.pdf"
literal "false"

\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
T\approx2\pi\sqrt{\frac{L}{g}}\frac{1}{\mathrm{agm}\left(1,cos\left(\frac{\theta}{2}\right)\right)}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
How do we calculate the agm()?
 Here’s some code written in C:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

%
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

