\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

{
\backslash
footnotesize
\backslash
begin{verbatim}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

#include <math.h>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

  // Return arithmetic-geometric mean (positive arguments).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

double agm (double a,
 double g)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

{
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

if (a <= 0 || g <= 0) { // domain error
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

return 0;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

for (;;) {
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

  double a0 = a;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

  double g0 = g;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

  a = (a0 + g0) / 2;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

  g = sqrt(a0 * g0);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

  if (a == g || fabs(a - g) >= fabs(a0 - g0)) {
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

    return a;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

    }
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

  }
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{verbatim}}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The idea is that we repeatedly loop around until the average of the arithmetic and geometric mean converges on the answer.
 The code is crude but effective,
 converging rapidly.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
If you pop it into Claude,
 the bot will correctly point out several defects—
it may misbehave with infinite values and those not-a-number (NAN);
 the return value of 0 is ambiguous;
 the test for convergence is subtle.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 And yes,
 it uses that N=N+1 paradigm.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here’s something similar,
 written in 
