\begin_layout Section
OOPS!
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As Armstrong points out,
 the fundamental difference between functional languages and Object-oriented programming (OOP) is that functional code avoids side effects and ‘mutable state’.
 If you further constrain things like,
 well,
 all variables,
 this cleans up not just your code space but your mental coding space.
 Or,
 as Armstrong says:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\size small
If you have referentially transparent code,
 if you have pure functions —
 all the data comes in its input arguments and everything goes out and leave no state behind —
 it’s incredibly reusable.”
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I think it was in 1987 that I watched OOP take off.
 One thing that bemused me about the spread of OOP at the time was the near-complete lack of evidence of superiority.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Or even equivalence,
 when it comes to things like code re-use.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset

 If OOP were a drug,
 I don’t think it would get past even the currently hugely compromised FDA.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
OOP clearly has a powerful place where (a) the hierarchical structure of objects closely maps to the structure of the space you’re trying to model.
 Unfortunately,
 it may be a suboptimal fit for (b) everything else,
 especially because the natural tendency in OOP is to spread your mutable state around,
 throughout your program.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Over the years,
 I’ve looked for decent studies comparing e.g.
 OOP and functional programming.
 You can find a few.
 Many of these are ‘toy’ comparisons.
 For example back in the 1990s,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Rachel Harrison et al."
target "https://digital-library.theiet.org/doi/10.1049/sej.1996.0030"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 compared C++ and SML (‘
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Standard ML"
target "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Standard_ML"
literal "false"

\end_inset

’ that is mainly functional),
 against twelve sets of algorithms,
 and found more errors with SML.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
There’s more here:
 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=BqWM2XwAAAAJ&hl=en"
target "https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=BqWM2XwAAAAJ&hl=en"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset

 The remaining ones are subject to controversy.
 For example,
 in 2014 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "Baishakhi Ray and colleagues"
target "https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/2635868.2635922"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 pulled 729 projects from GitHub (80 million lines of code,
 29k authors) and tried to tease out the effects of language on software quality.
 They found several effects—
benefits of strong typing (which we’ll cover in a later post),
 and a modest benefit of using functional languages,
 but the main effects they teased out related to other factors such as project size and team size.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The water is even more muddy today,
 because most popular languages boast “multi-paradigm” capabilities.
 You can use objects,
 functional,
 and often other options too.
 Sadly,
 this cuts to the heart of the problem—
excessive complexity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Complexity is still the enemy.
 With OOP,
 often “All I wanted was a banana”.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Failure
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I think the stand-out difference for Erlang is not write-once ‘variables’ or better management of things like mutexes (effectively,
 by 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "eliminating them"
target "https://learnxbyexample.com/erlang/mutexes/"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 Even the possible benefits of a functional approach are exceeded by something else.
 It’s Armstrong’s approach to error.
 Here’s a quote from his PhD thesis,
 once more:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quotation

\size small
The software systems in the case study are so reliable that the people operating these system are inclined to think that they are error-free.
 This is not the case,
 indeed software errors did occur at run-time but these errors were quickly corrected so that nobody ever noticed that the errors had occurred.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are two main ways we can approach software errors:
 terror or accommodation.
 The traditional approach is that the program clutches its pearls,
 cries out “Woe is me!” and dies catastrophically.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
This may even be mandatory.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset

 This is usually less than useful.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An alternative is simply to acknowledge that errors will occur,
 and to plan for and accommodate them.
 Unless code can be proven not to fail (under the required circumstances,
 which may be very broad),
 then it is more reasonable to plan for,
 trap and manage all sorts of errors,
 rather than to simply hope.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Erlang’s philosophy here is to have supervisor modules that are continually waiting for something to fail,
 and to rip all or almost all defensive error code out of the working code in those supervised modules.
 But even if we’re not going there,
 we can still respect,
 honour and plan for error.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
